}
\description{
Map a residue of one protein to the residue of another protein in the same column of Ensembl Compara's gene-tree alignment, or say why there is none, for a batch of queries.

Answers \strong{which mouse residue is human p53's Ser15?} Ensembl Compara has already aligned every
protein of each gene tree, across all its species, to build the tree. A residue's column in that
alignment is the published answer. This wraps mzLib's \code{ComparaGeneTreeAlignment} (mzLib #1465):
\code{Load} reads Compara's peptide alignment of every gene tree, keeping the canonical proteins of
your species' genes, and \code{MapResidue} answers each query: which residue of the target protein
sits in the same alignment column as residue \code{position} of the source protein. Local files only;
nothing is downloaded.
}
\details{
Every query gets exactly one outcome, mzLib's own name for it: \code{"Aligned"} (with the column, the
target position and its residue), \code{"GapInTarget"} (no target residue in that column - never the
nearest one), \code{"DifferentAlignments"} (the proteins are in different gene trees), or
\code{"SourceNotInAlignment"} / \code{"TargetNotInAlignment"} (that protein was not kept: only each gene's
canonical protein is aligned).

\strong{Positions are Ensembl's.} To start from a UniProt position, align the UniProt sequence to the
canonical Ensembl protein with \code{\link{proteins_align}} first, and align the target's Ensembl protein to
its UniProt sequence after. \code{proteins = TRUE} returns the Ensembl sequences for that.

\strong{A position outside the source protein fails the whole call.} mzLib's \code{MapResidue} throws for it
rather than returning an outcome, and mzLibR passes positions through unchecked, so keep them
within each source protein: with \code{proteins = TRUE}, a sequence's length is its last valid
position.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_residue_mappings}.

  \code{records} is a data.frame with one row per query, in the order given: \code{source_protein_id},
  \code{position} (the 1-based residue in the source, as given) and \code{target_protein_id}, as given;
  \code{outcome}, one of the five outcomes above; \code{column}, the source residue's 1-based column in the
  gene tree's alignment, \code{NA} when the two proteins share no alignment; \code{target_position}, the
  1-based position of the target's residue in that column, in the target's ungapped sequence,
  \code{NA} unless \code{"Aligned"}; and \code{target_residue}, the target's residue there, one letter, \code{NA}
  unless \code{"Aligned"}. It may be a different amino acid from the source's: a column pairs
  homologous positions, not identical ones.

  \code{row_count} counts the queries, one row each (queries). \code{outcome_counts} is a named vector,
  outcome to the queries with it (queries), every outcome present in mzLib's order, zeros
  included. \code{column_names} lists the table's columns.

  \code{alignment} is a list describing the alignment file: \code{source_file_name}; \code{sha256}, of the
  file's bytes as read (the compressed bytes for a \code{.gz}); \code{release} and \code{collection}, read from
  the file name (\code{NA} when the name carries none); \code{alignment_count}, the alignments in the file,
  one per gene tree, kept proteins or not (alignments); \code{protein_count}, the canonical proteins
  kept (proteins); and \code{restricted_to_gene_tree_content}, the sha256 of the gene-tree file whose
  canonical proteins were kept, the same as \code{gene_trees$sha256}.

  \code{gene_trees} is a list, as \code{\link{proteins_build_orthology}} reports it: \code{source_file_name},
  \code{sha256}, \code{release}, \code{collection}, \code{member_count}, the genes kept from the file (genes), and
  \code{restricted_to_gene_sets}, the gene sets' sha256s, or \code{NULL} when no gene set was given and
  every gene was kept.

  \code{gene_sets} is a data.frame, one row per species given (none without \code{gtfs} or \code{gene_sets}):
  \code{species}, \code{source_file_name}, \code{sha256}, \code{release}, \code{genome_build} and
  \code{genebuild_last_updated} (\code{NA} when the GTF's name or headers carry none), and \code{gene_count}, in
  genes, every biotype.

  \code{proteins_included} says whether \code{proteins = TRUE} was given. \code{proteins} is \code{NULL} without it;
  with it, a list: \code{row_count} (proteins kept), \code{column_names}, and \code{records}, a data.frame with
  one row per kept protein in order of protein id: \code{protein_id}, the Ensembl protein stable id;
  \code{alignment_index}, which alignment holds it (\strong{1-based in R}, in file order; two proteins share
  a column only when they share this); \code{tree_id}, its gene's Compara gene tree; and \code{sequence},
  its residues with the alignment's gaps removed, the sequence every position counts in.
  \code{setNames(m$proteins$records$sequence, m$proteins$records$protein_id)} gives the sequences by
  id, for \code{\link{proteins_align}}. \code{caveats} names this call's traps: gene trees read unrestricted,
  files named for different releases or for none, queries naming a protein the alignment did not
  keep.

}
\section{What each outcome means}{


\code{"Aligned"}: the target has a residue in the source residue's column; \code{column}, \code{target_position}
and \code{target_residue} are set. \code{"GapInTarget"}: the target has a gap in that column, so no residue
corresponds; \code{column} is set, the target's are \code{NA}. \code{"DifferentAlignments"}: the two proteins
are in different gene trees, so this alignment says nothing about them. It does not say they are
unrelated. \code{"SourceNotInAlignment"} and \code{"TargetNotInAlignment"}: that protein was not kept - not
in the file, not its gene's canonical protein, or outside your gene sets. Another isoform is
never mapped through the canonical one.

}
\section{The traps}{


\strong{A position past the end of the source protein fails the whole call} with mzLib's message (for
example "ENSP00000269305 has 393 residues."), as an \code{mzlib_usage_error}, and no row is returned,
not even for the queries that were fine. A source protein that was not kept is an outcome,
\code{"SourceNotInAlignment"}, checked before the position. \strong{Use Ensembl ids and Ensembl
positions}: a UniProt accession is \code{"SourceNotInAlignment"}, never an error. \strong{Use the alignment
and the gene trees of one release}: mzLib does not compare them, and a gene's canonical protein
and tree can change between releases. Keep Ensembl's file names, so the result can report the
release each one carries. \strong{Sharing a gene tree is not orthology}: a tree holds paralogs too,
so ask \code{\link{proteins_build_orthology}} which genes are orthologs, then map their residues here.
**Check \code{target_residue}** before carrying a modification across: a phosphosite on S is not a
site on A.

}
