\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{alignment}}{path; required. Compara's peptide alignments of its gene trees, emf/ensembl-compara/homologies/Compara.RELEASE.protein_COLLECTION.aa.fasta.gz (plain or .gz): one gapped multiple alignment per tree, as FASTA, separated by a // line. Read with ComparaGeneTreeAlignment.Load, which keeps only the canonical proteins of the genes kept from gene-trees, and checks every row's characters and width whether it is kept or not. 908 MB compressed for protein_default in release 116.}
\item{\code{gene_trees} (wire \code{--gene-trees})}{path; required. Compara's gene-tree membership dump, COLLECTION.GeneTree_content.default.eRELEASE.txt.gz (plain or .gz), read with ComparaGeneTreeContent.Load. It names each gene's tree and its canonical protein (the row flagged Y), which is the only protein of the gene the alignment holds.}
\item{wire \code{--species}}{string[]; default absent; range \code{>= 0 lines; each exactly three tab-separated fields}. Read from stdin (not an option), first: one line per species, the species' Ensembl production name (homo_sapiens), a tab, gtf or gene-set, a tab, and the path, exactly as genes orthology reads them. gtf is an Ensembl primary-assembly GTF, read with EnsemblGeneSet.LoadGtf; gene-set is the compact table mzLib's EnsemblGeneSetWriter makes from one, read with EnsemblGeneSetReader.Load. Their only use is ComparaGeneTreeContent.Load's restrictTo: only the genes in them are kept, and so only their canonical proteins. None (the stdin starts with --): mzLib's default, restrictTo null, keeps every gene in the gene-tree file and every canonical protein in the alignment, millions for the vertebrate collection, and a caveat says so. A species listed twice, a kind other than gtf or gene-set, or a missing file is a usage error. \emph{Not an argument here: rendered from gtfs and gene_sets, two named character vectors from Ensembl production name to path, one stdin line per species (none: mzLib keeps every gene).}}
\item{\code{queries}}{string[]; required; range \code{>= 1 line; each exactly three tab-separated fields}. Read from stdin (not an option), after a line holding only --: one query per line, the source protein's Ensembl stable id, a tab, a 1-based residue position in it, a tab, and the target protein's id (ENSP00000269305, 15, ENSMUSP00000104298). One row per query, in input order, duplicates included. The position is parsed as an integer and passed to MapResidue unchecked; the bridge does not compare it with the protein's length.}
\item{\code{proteins}}{flag; default \code{FALSE}. Also return the kept proteins (ComparaGeneTreeAlignment.Proteins: id, alignment, tree and ungapped sequence), so a protein's Ensembl sequence can be aligned to a UniProt sequence with proteins align without a second file. Absent: proteins is null.}
}
}
