\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item A column pairs positions that Compara's alignment puts together, which is homology, not identity: the target residue may be a different amino acid, so check target_residue before carrying a modification across (a phosphosite on S is not a site on A).
\item Only each gene's canonical protein is in the alignment (the gene-tree row flagged Y). A query naming another isoform's Ensembl id is SourceNotInAlignment or TargetNotInAlignment, never mapped through the canonical one. To start from a UniProt position, align the UniProt sequence to the canonical Ensembl protein with proteins align first, then map, then align the target's canonical protein to its UniProt sequence: three steps, each of which can meet a gap.
\item A gap is GapInTarget, never the nearest residue: no residue of the target sits in that column.
\item DifferentAlignments means the two proteins are in different gene trees, so this alignment says nothing about them. It does not say the genes are not homologous, nor that they are orthologs when they share one: a tree holds paralogs as well, so read orthology from genes orthology.
\item mzLib's MapResidue throws for a position outside the source protein instead of returning an outcome, so one such query fails the whole call, and the bridge does not check lengths for it. Keep positions inside each source protein; \code{--proteins} gives each kept protein's sequence.
\item mzLib does not compare the alignment's release with the gene-tree file's. Use the two files of one release: a gene's canonical protein and its tree can change between releases. The envelope reports the release each file name carries, and a caveat says when they differ or one is absent.
\item Every input is read whole, and the alignment keeps only the canonical proteins of the genes kept, so give the species' gene sets: with none, every gene in the gene-tree file is kept (millions for the vertebrate collection), and so every canonical protein of the alignment.
}
}
\section{Performance}{
ComparaGeneTreeAlignment.Load reads the whole alignment file once, checking every row, and is the cost; mapping a query afterwards is a binary search. For human, mouse and rat at release 116 (64,114 proteins kept from 54,308 alignments), mzLib #1465 reports about 33 s to load, and this bridge measured 55 s for the whole call on 2026-10-28, reading the three GTFs, the gene-tree file and the alignment. Give all of a batch's queries to one call. Neither load takes a degree of parallelism, so there is no \code{--threads}.
Every call starts one bridge process, which costs a .NET start-up before any work. 
}
